<p>China’s annual “Two Sessions” political meetings begin this week in Beijing. Analysts expect the government to signal that it can live with slightly slower growth, while also laying out priorities for 2026–2030 tied to the 15th five-year plan. Many expect Premier Li Qiang to announce a 2026 growth target in the 4.5%–5% range, alongside pledges to lift household consumption while continuing heavy investment in high-tech industries—two goals China has long pursued side by side. The debate comes as China works through a prolonged housing downturn and ongoing challenges for young people trying to find jobs. Several sources point to strong exports but weak domestic consumption, and flag related concerns such as deflationary pressure and industrial overcapacity—complications for Beijing’s push for what it calls “high quality development”. Beyond economic planning, the sessions are expected to highlight China’s push for technology self-reliance and its broader defence priorities as competition with the US intensifies. They also arrive as officials stress financial risk control and policy stability while China adjusts to more modest near-term growth.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DRizugTBmJXSR8-08sohU6xhCefHAhip_gB3YDm5q0vv7xJCyBlv3nac7EzU33ENruSM0DAw34-2M3vjViw0n8tP6AvT3VWZvD4u-nRHm3ZoM1YK0AiYgpraev9c-OO95eG8wjQzCl_Z6rviiAaXqBiwqe72qIebaZxT3QR_5mU3sn89gdx_42avkfUadlBM' alt='Delegates gather for China’s National People’s Congress in Beijing.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Overcapacity leeway: Analysts say accepting slightly slower growth could open a bit more space—though not a game-changer—for Beijing to rein in industrial overcapacity and lean less on exports.</li><li>Plan framing: Coverage expects the 2026–2030 blueprint to keep “high quality development” and tech-led “new productive forces” at the center of economic policy.</li><li>Risk and corruption messaging: Ahead of the sessions, senior officials have again stressed defusing financial risks and tackling political corruption.</li><li>Media window: The Two Sessions remain one of the few moments when global media get a close-up view of China’s top lawmakers in Beijing.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chinese policymakers (via adviser): A policy adviser said officials want to do more to boost household consumption while still prioritising tech-led “new productive forces,” and said the growth goal may be framed as a range rather than a single number. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/china-poised-to-trim-growth-ambitions-in-five-year-plan/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li><li>AP/ABC News analysis: The report pointed to headline-grabbing tech displays — including kung-fu fighting robots and self-parking cars — but said a prolonged housing slump and weak job prospects for young people are constraining growth. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/chinas-economic-ambitions-hit-limits-growth-national-congress-130699545'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>The Guardian: The preview focused on defence planning and technology self-reliance amid US competition, and said a military purge has cast a shadow over the political meetings. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/03/china-two-sessions-president-xi-economy-defence-technology'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/china-poised-to-trim-growth-ambitions-in-five-year-plan/'>China poised to trim growth ambitions in five-year plan</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/chinas-economic-ambitions-hit-limits-growth-national-congress-130699545'>China&#x27;s economic ambitions hit limits to growth as its national congress meets</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/chinas-economic-ambitions-hit-limits-to-growth-as-its-national-congress-meets/article70697980.ece'>China&#x27;s economic ambitions hit limits to growth as its National Congress meets</a> - thehindu.com</li>